  "order": 37,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "td9cpzz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "WORK, ENERGY AND POWER"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Work is scalar and defined as W = F · d · cos(θ), occurring only when force causes displacement.",
        "Kinetic energy (KE = ½mv²) depends on motion, while potential energy (PE = mgh or ½kx²) depends on position or shape.",
        "The Work-Energy Theorem establishes that net work done on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy (W = ΔKE).",
        "Power is the rate at which work is performed or energy is converted (P = W / t).",
        "Mechanical energy is conserved under conservative forces, whereas non-conservative forces dissipate energy as heat or sound."
      ],
      "id": "2g6fpyr"
    },
